# Feature layer packages

Inside `features/<name>`, put classes in:

```text
org.devbot.bookmymovie.<name>.api
org.devbot.bookmymovie.<name>.domain
org.devbot.bookmymovie.<name>.data
```

| Package | Allowed |
|---------|---------|
| `api` | `@AppApi` / `@WebApi` controllers, HTTP DTOs, `@RestControllerAdvice`, web/security `@Configuration` |
| `domain` | `@Service`, use cases, business rules |
| `data` | `@Entity`, Spring Data repositories |

```java
// BAD — entity in api, controller in data
package org.devbot.bookmymovie.movie.api;
@Entity
public class Movie {}

// GOOD
package org.devbot.bookmymovie.movie.data;
@Entity
public class Movie {}

package org.devbot.bookmymovie.movie.api;
@AppApi
public class MovieAppController {
    @GetMapping("/movies")
    public List<MovieSummaryResponse> list() { return List.of(); }
}
```

Use `@AppApi` (`/api/v1/app`) and `@WebApi` (`/api/v1/web`) from `org.devbot.bookmymovie.shared.api`. Map resources on methods only — do not add a second type-level `@RequestMapping`.

- `api` calls `domain`. `domain` calls `data` in the **same** feature.
- Do not put JPA entities on controller return types; map to DTOs in `api`.
- Auth security config (`SecurityFilterChain`, filters) goes in `org.devbot.bookmymovie.auth.api`, not another feature and not `core`.
- `@ManyToOne` / `@OneToMany` are allowed inside the jar and toward a module this feature depends on (`show` → `Movie`; `booking` → `User` / `Show`).
